  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1243 AM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nToday - Tuesday: On and Off Chances for Rain, Dry Periods \n\nA weak upper level shortwave trough overhead is interacting \nwith relatively deep moisture west of the Mississippi River \ncreating some isolated light showers. Additional development is \nexpected through the early morning hours as additional mid-level\nmoisture makes its way into the region. With generally drier \nair east of the river, most activity will remain across SE \nMinnesota and NE Iowa through the morning hours before \ndissipating as synoptic lift from the exiting upper trough \nweakens. Generally dry conditions will prevail for late Sunday \nthrough Monday with temperatures ranging from ranging from the \nmid 60s to the mid 70s. \n\nFor the early portions of the work week, our area will be in \nbetween two primary weather systems: a low pressure system \nacross the northeastern US and strong trough across the \nSouthwestern CONUS. This will leave our area in a realm of \nincreasing southwesterly flow aloft. Deep moisture is also \nexpected to be on an uptick moving into Tuesday as it surges \nnorthward ahead of the trough. This deep moisture combined with \nembedded shortwave impulses will allow for the development of \nshowers and potentially thunderstorms across the region Tuesday \nthrough Tuesday night. Precipitable water values could certainly\nexceed 1.75 inches, maybe even nearing the 2 inch threshold, \nthrough the course of the event. Current WPC forecasts show QPF \namounts generally between 0.5-0.75 inches for much of the area \nwith some locations approaching or even just barely exceeding \nthe 1 inch threshold from 12Z Tuesday to 12Z Wednesday. While \nthe 90th percentile rainfall amounts from the LREF do highlight\nthe potential for some locations to see an inch or more, \nconfidence in this occurring is still fairly low as appreciable \nlow level forcing might be hard to come by (certainly hard to \nmake out at this time) and moisture quality will be a factor. \n\nRest of Next Week: Daily Rain Chances May Continue\n\nThe remainder of this forecast period is highly uncertain as it\nwill depend upon the evolution of the low pressure center \nassociated with the synoptic scale trough as well as how this \nfeature combines with the remnants of now Hurricane Polo. \nEnsemble guidance along with deterministic forecast models \nindicate plenty of differences in timing and location in the \naxis of better rain chances for Wednesday and beyond. This is \nshown really well in the NBM PoP values, which keep at least low\nto medium (20-50%) chances for rain through Friday. The current\nexpectation is that the heaviest rainfall will stay south of \nthe Upper Midwest, but there are outlier solutions that produce \n2+ inches of rainfall during the later half of this upcoming \nweek. Temperatures are forecast to remain near average to end \nthe month of September.",
